course: dictionary
lesson: 15882
title: Unintelligent: not clever, or not sensible
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word unintelligent has a small part at the front: un-, which means not. So unintelligent means not intelligent, which means not clever or not showing good thinking. «The unintelligent choice was to run across the busy road without looking.» Look at the clues: running across a busy road without looking is a risky thing to do, so unintelligent here means not sensible.
explain: You can use unintelligent about a choice or an action, and about a person's thinking. When it describes a choice, it means the choice was not sensible. «It was unintelligent to leave the door open in the snow.» The snow and the open door are the clues: a sensible person would shut the door.
explain: Be careful when you use it about a person. Calling someone unintelligent is a judgement about their thinking, and it is unkind. Writers often use a polite word like unintelligent in formal writing instead of a rude word. «Calling someone unintelligent is unkind, even when they make a mistake.»
fact: Unintelligent means not clever, or not showing good thinking or understanding.
fact: When it describes a choice or action, unintelligent means not sensible.
fact: The un- at the front of a word means not, so unintelligent is the opposite of intelligent.
fact: «Unintelligent» is a formal word, often used in polite writing instead of rude words.
fact: Calling a person unintelligent is unkind, because it judges how they think.
exercise: «It was unintelligent to run across the busy road without looking.» What does unintelligent mean here? | Very fast | Quite funny | *Not sensible | Very kind
exercise: Mia says, «Unintelligent means clever.» Is she right? | Yes, it means happy | No, it means very loud | *No, it means not clever | Yes, it means very clever
exercise: Which word means the opposite of intelligent? | slow | happy | kind | *unintelligent
test: «The robot made an unintelligent decision and rolled off the edge.» What does unintelligent mean here? | *Not sensible | Very shiny | Fast and loud | Lost in the dark
test: Why do writers sometimes use a polite word like unintelligent? | To be rude | To show a joke | To make the story longer | *To sound formal and avoid hurtful words
exam: Why is it unkind to call someone unintelligent? | It is a fast and loud shout | It means they are very rich | It is only used in maths | *It judges a person's thinking, and it can hurt them
